Greetings-

	I have been given the lucky task of upgrading our OS from version 3.0 to
version 3.2D, but am running into a bit of a snag and was wondering if any
of the UNIX gurus out there could see exactly where I went wrong.  I am
aware that I cannot go directly from 3.0 to 3.2D, and so acquired the 3.2
upgrade (which is supposed to include 3.2C, though the techs told me that I
didn't need to go from 3.2 to 3.2C, but could go directly from 3.2 to 3.2D.
	I started by cleaning up any old or outdated accounts, then backed up the
entire system using tar.  After that, I booted to single user and ran the
/sbin/installupdate.  The upgrade appeared to be flawless until the first
time I went to log on and found that it claimed it could not find the OSF
Base PAK.   Figuring that I must have done something wrong (well, it is my
first UNIX upgrade) I then restored from the tar backup and rebooted. 
Everything was back to normal.  I then went directly to the manual and
followed each and every step, even being so anal retentive as to stop and
check off each step as it was completed.  Again the upgrade appeared to be
flawless until I logged in the first time..., same thing..., no OSF Base
PAK found.  
	I then checked the upgrade logs to see if there were any errors during the
upgrade, though it didn't show any.  I then checked the License
Manufacturing Facility.  According to the status column in the lmf list,
everything is terminated.  Based on this information, I am guessing that as
long as the PAK was installed prior to the termination date, the
applications (OS included) will work fine.  However, based on upgrades I
have performed on other operating systems (Win3.1, Win95, WinNT, and Macs)
anytime an *upgrade* is performed, the required *keys* are carried forward
with the upgrade.  This does not appear to be the case here.
	Suggestions?  Ridicule?  Scorn?  At this point any of the 3, as long as
they point me in the right direction, would be welcome.

Rob



