I have a bizarre problem with decladebug that I have not seen listed before.  It
used to work on our systems, then all of a sudden stopped working.  I have not
succeeded in determining the cause.

I compile an application with Digital C++ v5.1 on a node running Digital Unix
v3.2D-2 as follows:

	$ cxx -g -o testme.cxx51 testme.c

If I simply run the program "testme.cxx51", I get the output:

	$ testme.cxx51
	Hello world

If I try to run the program with DEC ladebug v4.0-7, I get the output:

	$ decladebug testme.cxx51

	Welcome to the DECladebug Debugger Version 4.0-7
	------------------ 
	object file name: testme.cxx51 
  >>>	Reading symbolic information ...Hello world
	Internal Error: could not start debuggee
	Could not start process for testme.cxx51
	No image loaded ... Recovering ... 
	(decladebug) quit
                                                                                
Notice the line marked ">>>".  It actually >contains< the output from the
testme.cxx51 program.  In other words, it looks like my program ran but ladebug
was not able to "catch" it.

I had similar problems with an earlier version of ladebug.  I can also
duplicate the problem on a D.Unix v3.2b node running Digital C++ v5.3 and
ladebug v4.0-7.  The crazy thing is that DEC ladebug >used< to work (many months
ago) and I am not sure what broke it.  I did install AFS at some point (on both
nodes) and had to rebuild the kernel, but I am not sure if that is the cause of
the problem or not.

If anyone has any ideas, please let me know.  Thanks.


[Here is the contents of testme.c:

	#include <stdio.h>

	int main( int argc, char *argv[] )
	{
	  printf( "Hello world\n" );
	
	}
]

