Arnold Vogelsang wrote:

> I am about to debug a substantially large
> application, consisting of FORTRAN (mainly) and C
> sources. When trying to use DECladebug to debug the
.
.
.
> Warning: illegal aux index 3 for file #29
> <some_commonblock_name>
> 
> Finally, a memory fault leads to a core dump. The
> DECladebug prompt has not been seen yet.
> 
> Any ideas what to do? Thanks in advance.

Arnold,

You need to upgrade your ladebug version.  3.0-9 is several years old,
before it played nicely with Fortran.  I personally have done 2 years
worth of work adding Fortran support.  The problem you are seeing is due
to block data subroutines, which ladebug did not know how to handle. 
You should try to get at least version 4.0-14A.  If not, try 3.0-16
(from Digital UNIX 3.2), which should have better support.  Try and get
4.0-14(or A) if possible.  It is available from the support center.  If
you can't get it there, then we can make some arrangements to get one to
you (but it is not easy.)

Are you using dxladebug too?  There is a kernel patch you need if you
are.

Regards,
Tim
