In article <3270C6CC.39E7@navier.ist.utl.pt>, Jorge Prates <Jorge.Prates@navier.ist.utl.pt> writes:

># fsck -p /dev/rrz0h
>/dev/rrz0h: /dev/rrz0h: BAD SUPER BLOCK: MAGIC NUMBER WRONG

Some of the things that might have happened:

1.  The partition table has changed with respect to the old
    group of file systems.  In this case, the location of
    the H partition doesn't match where the file system still
    (maybe) is.  You may find the file system intact if you
    can get the partition table recovered.

2.  You selected AdvFS instead of UFS and it scribbled over
    the superblock.  The first backup superblock is always
    at 32, so you might try it.  Failing that, newfs has an
    option that will echo the superblock locations without
    overwriting the file system.  If you can use the right
    parameters for the command, you can try different superblocks
    off that list.

3.  You scribbled over parts of the file system.  As with #2
    you may be able to find an intact superblock from the old
    file system to use with fsck.  If not, UFS salvage takes
    lots of work...
>
>/dev/rrz0h: UNEXPECTED INCONSISTENCY; RUN fsck MANUALLY.
>

