Last week I upgraded my AlphaServer 1000 to Digital Unix 4.0 from 3.2C.

My backups have thrown fits since.

I'm backing up across our network to a Sun SparcServer 1000's 4mm DAT tape.

Previously, my working backup routine looked like:
-------------------------------------------------------------------
PATH=/usr/bin:/usr/sbin ; export PATH
rdump -0cuf fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n /
rdump -0cuf fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n /usr
rdump -0cuf fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n /home
rsh fau.edu mt offline
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Output from the first rdump command is:
==========================================
# rdump -0cuf fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n /
rdump: Dumping from host aura.irm.fau.edu
rdump: Date of this level 0 dump: Mon Oct 28 14:24:02 1996 EST
rdump: Date of last level 0 dump: the start of the epoch 
rdump: Dumping /dev/rrz0a (/) to /dev/rmt/0n on host fau.edu
rdump: Mapping (Pass I) [regular files]
rdump: Mapping (Pass II) [directories]
rdump: Remote system is not known to be compatible for magtape functions,
 output being treated as regular file
rdump: Estimate: 53514 tape blocks on disk
The available blocks(4059),is less than the estimated blocks(53514)
Dump is being aborted
rdump: SIGTERM received 
rdump: The ENTIRE dump is aborted
===========================================

All subsequent attempts at tweaking the tape size, blocking factors, density,
etc.  result in the same error message.  I have not been able to convince the
command that the tape is bigger than it assumes. Commands I have tried:

# rdump  -0cuB -f fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n -d 56000  -s 5000 -S 2000000 /
# rdump -f fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n -0cu -BS 2000000 /
# rdump  -0cuB -f fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n -S 2000000 /      
# rdump -f fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n -0cuBS 2000000 / 
# rdump -0Ecu -d 54000 -f fau.edu:/dev/rmt/0n /

All result in the same error messages.

Nothing has changed on the Sun.  I backup two other platforms (Sun, IBM) to 
the Sun DAT tape with no problems.

Any suggestions at this point would be great.

