organization: U.S. Online, Inc.
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


> > We are running Digital Unix 3.2D and a heavely loaded
> > (1M+ hits per day) Apache server.
> > 
> > We are often getting the error:
> > 
> > access to (snip)/cgi-bin/cashier failed (snip), reason: 
> > couldn't spawn child process
> > 
> > I can not for the life of me remember what needs to be
> > changed.
> > 
> > Assistance greatly appreciated!
> > 
> > -Steve Camp
> 
> Try raising the maximum processes allowed per user in /etc/sysconfigtab.
> 
> --Tony
> 

Dear Mr.Steve

	Since you seem to be saying that you have a million hit every day,
you may want to increase the overall system resources too, apart from the
max-proc-per-user attribute(Like Mr.Tony had pointed out in his response).
I would say you increase

1. the maxusers value to 64 based on your memory and License
    requirements.
2. The system default of 64 for max-proc-per-user is only applicable for
non-root
    users. So if your programs are daemons that run in root privilege,
increasing
    max-proc-per-user may not help.

The system wide process limits like nproc are a function of the maxusers
setting. So increasing maxusers to 64 may allot more system resources
like more vnodes, more processes, more callouts, etc. When you increase
this, then you got to tune other stuff like File systems to take care of
Disk
hits and memory hits. If you are using UFS file systems, you may want to
tune
the UBC buffer cache usage etc based on your system performance. It would
be advisable, if you can go for some serious System tuning based on the
performance analysis that you have done using some tools like

Polycenter Performance Advisor from Digital

These are just my thoughts. A simple change of maxusers may go a long way
for you

Hope this info helps you

Sincerely

Sukumar Alagappan



