In article <01bbc0de$ca264900$283c9fa8@bferjulian.lss.emc.com>, "Bruce Ferjulian" <Ferjulian_Bruce@emc.com> writes:

>
>Question:
>	  How do I get the system to recognize the disk, Digital UNIX is
>supposed to be open isn't it. There must be a way to see a disk that is
>not named "RZ" something.

It would help to know what version you have and what scu *does*
show about the device.  From about V1.2 on DEC OSF/1, now called
Digital UNIX, the SCSI driver has been quite happy to find any
direct access (disk) or sequential access (tape) device on a 
SCSI bus and let you use through the base system class drivers.
The kernel code that counts accesses and bytes transferred has,
in some versions, needed clues from the configuration file that
a disk was available.  The "device disk" entries do this.  But
this hasn't been necessary since about V3.0.

My only guess is that you created the wrong special device file
or the disk isn't working.  The device "rz1" is bus 0, target 1,
LUN 0.  While an "show device" will get the Inquiry data, the
device may not have to pass Test Unit Ready for that to work.
Use "tur" in scu, to see if the device is ready.

Oh, for Alpha based systems DEC OSF/1 systems, V1.2 was the 
beginning of time.
