content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Stanford University School of Earth Sciences
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 2.02 (Macintosh; I; 68K)


Matthew Barton wrote about problems getting rdump under Digital Unix
4.0 to backup to a 4 mm DAT drive on a Sun system.

May I suggest an alternative?

Instead of using the "rdump" command, why not just use plain "dump"
piped to an "rsh" remote shell that runs the "dd" program on the 
Sun system to put the data on tape?  For example, if you were logged
into the Digital UNIX system and wanted to dump /usr to the tape device
on the Sun (assume it is named /dev/tape and the sun is named "sun" --
put the correct values for your system into the command):
     dump 0uf - /usr | rsh sun 'dd of=/dev/tape obs=10k'
The "obs" parameter tells dd to reblock to 10 kilobyte blocks, 
the standard for dump tapes.  This is needed because rsh pushes
the data across the network in odd-sized packets of <= 1500 bytes.
You will need to make the root accounts on the two systems 
equivalent, of course, using /.rhosts (but this is needed for
rdump as well, isn't it?).

In my testing, I have found that the 'dump | rsh dd' pipe is 
about three times as fast as using rdump.

-- 
Phil Farrell, Computer Systems Manager
Stanford University School of Earth Sciences
farrell@pangea.stanford.edu



