In article <yeak9sa7grq.fsf@fire.syd.dms.CSIRO.AU>, Hugues Talbot
<talbot@fire.syd.dms.csiro.au> wrote:

>         I have to read a 4GB+ single file full of complex
> data. Solaris 2.5 on a Sparc 20 chokes on it. Before I even attempt on
> other platforms, does anyone know if DEC Unix (3.2) has a file size
> limit ?

The specific answer is in the Digital UNIX Software Product description. 
In general, the file size limit is the same as the file system size limit,
which as of 3.2x is 128GB if I remember correctly.

>         While I'm at it, what about NFS ? if I can read that file
> on the alpha workstation, can I NFS export the partition and have
> Solaris read it ? (all the software I need to run on the file is
> Solaris only, unfortunately...).

Yes, you can export the partition.
And sorry, your Solaris system will still choke when trying to read the 4GB+
file. Those 32bit file pointers on Solaris only let you access 2GB at most. 
You can open the 4GB file on Solaris that you've exported from the Alpha
via NFS, but it'll only be able to get at the first 2 GB of it.



