Subject: Re: Inode allocation in AdvFS
Date: 1996/10/19
Message-ID: <549kd6$gt8@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 190743368
distribution: world
references: <547m6l$cp7@polaris.eurocontrol.fr>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
keywords: inode allocation policy
reply-to: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



In article <547m6l$cp7@polaris.eurocontrol.fr>, Ollivier.Robert@eurocontrol.fr (Ollivier Robert) writes:
>
>spare_domain#spare  1645640   32  1640784  0%   4 5589656     0%   /spare
>
>Why does it have 5.5 _millions_ of inodes ? Even with an allocation policy
>of 512 bytes per inodes, that's too much.

AdvFS dynamically allocates the on-disk data structure that passes
for an inode.  To the best of my knowledge there are only two things
that will create that many:

1.  Need.  A some point in its life, the file system needed that many
    or close to that many.  Space for the inode-like data structure
    is allocated in large contiguous extends.  The smallest likely
    extent is 1 MB.  I don't know if there is a largest, beyond the
    limits of having enough space for it.  However, when the space
    is extended, a new extent the size of the first is allocated.
    If that is very large (and historically there are reasons it
    could be) and only a few got used, you'd have a lot of wasted
    space.

2.  Pre-allocation & History lessons.  AdvFS does lots of things very
    well.  One thing it has never done well is support I/O loads that
    delete and create many small files.  Such loads quickly fragment
    the free space and as the need for more inode-like data structures
    grows it becomes impossible to allocate them.  The result is often
    a failure to create a file even though plenty of free space exists;
    just not contiguous free space.  One common application that causes
    this sort of load is USENET News.

    One of the earliest recommended work-arounds to the problem was
    to pre-allocate empty files and then delete them when the domain
    was created.  This would allocate the necessary extents when there
    was plenty of contiguous free space.  People still ran out, but
    not as often.

Assuming that df(1) is reporting the correct values, either #1 or #2
is why you have so many of these data structures.

>
>Is there a way to get a more decent allocation policy ?

Backup, recreate the file system and don't pre-allocate before
you restore.  However, if you think you might ever need that many
files, give the process a second thought.  As for the average file
size being less than a sector; empty files need homes too...

>
