organization: Your Organization
content-type: Text/Plain; charset=US-ASCII
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Does anybody know how to control sending of 'keep-alives' on 
tcp-connections 
(OSF/1 - alpha platforms) ?

The problem arises when programming tcp at the socket-level (in C). 

Consider, as an example, a proces running under OSF (on an alphabox) 
which communicates with a proces running on (say) a PC via a connected 
pair of tcp/ip-sockets.
If the pc is turned of (or explodes or whatever) the socket-software
on the PC won't have time to tell that it's going down and the 
socket-software on the alphabox will not recieve any notification that 
the connection is 
broken. 
To deal with this one needs to set up the tcp-connection so that it is 
periodically probed
by sending 'keep-alives'. This is indeed possible in C, under OSF. In 
the file 
  
/usr/include/netinet/tcp_timers.h 

two constants are defined:

#define TCPTV_KEEP_IDLE (120*60*PR_SLOWHZ)  /* dflt time before probing 
*/
#define TCPTV_KEEPINTVL ( 75*PR_SLOWHZ)   /* default probe interval */ 

and this, as far as I understand, means that the first keep-alive will 
be sendt  
2 hours after establishing the connection, and then keep-alives will be 
sendt every 
75 seconds.

The question is then: How does one change this? How can you make the 
probing start as soon
as the connection is established?





