Andrew Smith <ccasmith@cc.uq.edu.au> writes:

>stty pass8

There are a couple of options you should look at. Do a stty -a.
You should see cs8 set, and istrip set. (This means there is no
hyphen next to them: e.g.

-parenb -parodd cs8 -cstopb hupcl cread -clocal -crtscts 
-ignbrk brkint -ignpar -parmrk -inpck istrip -inlcr -igncr icrnl -iuclc 

>which enabled passing through 8 bit ASCII.

Umm, there's no such thing as 8 bit ASCII. ASCII by definition is a
7 bit codeset. I assume you need support for 8bit codesets such as 
ISO8859-1. Yes?

Also, you'll want to be careful of rlogin. By default rlogin sets
a 7 bit datapath, there's an option to override this, and in fact
I just alias rlogin to override this at all times.

rlogin='rlogin -8'

>This no longer seems to exist on Digital Unix. Instead, there is a whole
>lot of incomprehensible locale support, which involves loading
>additional software sets.

Sorry you find them so incomprehensible. I'd be happy to listen to
any suggestions you might have on making them more comprehensible.

--
