Well, this is starting to look like silly willy-waving when all
I had tried to do was point out a spiteful warping of words.

Personally, I am developing cross-platform software in C++.  At
the moment I use WinNT and Win95 for Win32 stuff and Linux for
pthread stuff and UNIXism.

What is it good for?

Well, aside from the fact that the tools you mention for 'Windows'
are all more stable (and adequately fast), I find it to be an
excellent C++ and Java development platform.

My NT box always runs an SMTP mail server, its a file server to my
other machines, it runs several different DBMS systems (SQL Server,
Watcom, Omniscience).  It hosts a decent Netscape v3 port, and
Symantec Cafe.  And Visual C++, Borland C++, Watcom C++, and
Symantec C++.  And Purify.

If I bothered to upgrade to NT4 (which I am resisting mostly because
a) my wife prefers the existing interface and b) I'd like to make
sure that my software works on 3.51 as well as 4.0) then I'd have
a further choice of VJ++ and IE3, both of which are competitive.
Whether I'd change to either remains to be seen.

So, NT is good for a lot of things.  If I had to choose, I'd keep
just NT - it hosts software that I can't get equivalents to (or at
least equivalents with the same functionality or support or
future vendor support or whatever) and its just a comfy environment
in which I can do all sorts of things without worries about stability
or software compatibility.

Linux is clearly very fast and efficient but it is a struggle to
get an environment together.  For example, I'm having to buy
a new ethernet card, and Crisp 5.1 has display anomalies
on the console.  And in truth I'd like a decent alternative to
gcc, though it looks like I'll get by.

If I had to choose just one box, Linux and Win95 would both go.
But I don't have to choose, so I run whatever I need to get things
done.  Once I'm happy with the Linux port, I'll ramp up onto
Solaris and OS/2, too.

