In article <54gd1o$h4s@garcia.efn.org> davidw@efn.org (David Welton) writes:

>   The thing that gets me about NT is the whole scam with the server and
>   workstation versions.  Apparently, by changing one file it is possible to
>   make the cheap version function like the expensive version.

I'm not sure I would charaterize this as a 'scam'.  All you're really
saying is that you know of a physical way to get around the licensing
restrictions.

This type of thing is certainly nothing new.  Unix
vendors charge different amounts for a many-user license
vs. single-user.  They charge different amounts depending on the
expense of the hardware on which it's running.  I recall a story in
which a company decided to upgrade their IBM card-reader to a faster
version.  The IBM tech came out with a single screwdriver, nothing
else.  He took off a cover, revealing a belt installed on two
different-diameter pulleys.  He removed the pulleys from their shafts,
swapped them, and slid them back in.  Bam, the reader was twice the
speed.  Three minutes, no new parts.  Processors have been known to
contain NO-OPs in the microcode in order to slow it down, so that a
single fast processor design and manufacturing stream could feed two
different market segments.  It seems odd to many at first, but when
you get past the assumption that products should be priced according
to development and manufacturing costs exclusively, it makes sense.

	Steve
