My mainframe folk tell me that they have this wonderful
facility that allows them to span a file across HUNDREDS
of tapes.  Their program does not have to deal with it, it just
opens the file on disk (or so it thinks), and the operator
is prompted to place the needed tapes into the drive(s).
The program then reads the data sequentially, and when 
it hits the end of tape, the next one is loaded if it is there.
If not, the operator is prompted to insert the correct one.

Same thing for output, it just uses a different drive, while
the program thinks it's a file.

As long as there is no seeking, pure sequential access,
this works.

I laugh, and say, who cares, I just keep all the data online, it's
a lot cheaper in my environment, I'm not paying MF disk 
prices.  They laugh back, and ask me if I have a terrabyte 
of  storage on line, because that is the quantity required for
the current operation.

I go scurrying to the net, because I've been told
to implement the same type of environment under Unix.
How?  Any products out there that sit between the OS
and the file system and will handle all the tape 
management?  Run under what OS?  Costs how much?
Please help.  Don't let the mainframers laugh at me
anymore.

Please cc to email to "broomberg@ccgroupnet.com".
Thanks.



