Keith DeLong (keith@alpha.delta.edu) wrote:

: Is there a quick way (inside a shell script) to ask the system which
: IP (or domain name) a user has logged in from?  Kind of like "who -M" does
: but only the host name for the user running the script.

Hi,

this is what I do in some shell-scripts that automagically set the DISPLAY 
variable to the machine the user logged onto from. Note that when using tcsh 
there is a variable REMOTEHOST that solves the task for you automatically
(if you've set up tcsh that way):

### O/S, hostname, etc.
setenv SYSTEM `uname`
setenv HOSTNAME `hostname | awk -F. '{print $1}'`
set TTYENV="`tty |& cat`"

### Most tcsh (but not all!) set REMOTEHOST to the machine we're logging in
### from. If that's the case: use it! If not: do some logic to find the
### name we're logging in from. TTYENV contains either the tty or "not a
### tty"

if ( ${?REMOTEHOST} != 0) then
  setenv RHOSTNAME $REMOTEHOST
else
  if ( "$TTYENV" == "not a tty" ) then
    setenv RHOSTNAME $HOSTNAME
  else
    setenv TTY `tty | awk -F/ '{print $NF}'`
    setenv RHOSTNAME `finger $user|grep from|grep $TTY|head -1|awk '{a=$NF} {FS=":"} {split(a,b)} {FS="."} {split(b[1],f)} {print f[1]}'`
    if ($RHOSTNAME == "") setenv RHOSTNAME $HOSTNAME
    if ($RHOSTNAME == "from") setenv RHOSTNAME $HOSTNAME
    if ($RHOSTNAME == \:0) setenv RHOSTNAME $HOSTNAME
  endif
endif
if ($RHOSTNAME == \:0.0) setenv RHOSTNAME $HOSTNAME

The central line in the script is the `finger...` line. Works for me
on Ultrix 4.2, Ultrix 4.4, OSF 3.2C, HP-UX 9.5.0, HP-UX 10.1.0, Solaris
(note that not all commands in the awk script do something on the different
machines; I need all of them only in the case of HP-UX but they don't hurt).


Cheers

