In article <53g7ff$3k78@news.doit.wisc.edu>, Brad Spencer
<brad@chem.wisc.edu> wrote:

> When I use the Digital bindsetup script to set up a system as the master name
> server for my domain it creates entries like:
> 
> 
> jchemed_22              IN      A               144.92.39.22
> "hosts-21"              IN      CNAME           jchemed_22
> 
> where the "hosts-22" entry is created by the script and has no relationship to
> any name I actually wish to create.  Does anyone know why Digital does this?
> Is there a different script that doesn't do this available someplace?  

This behavior has been around since the ULTRIX days. Never understood what
the use of it was except the number probably indicated the line number in
the original source file or some such thing.

When I did Digital UNIX DNS zonefile maintenance, I ditched bindsetup in
favor of setting the named.boot file up manually (it's not rocket science
to do it manually) and using h2n (the utility described in the Liu and
Albitz 'DNS and BIND' book published by O'Reilly) to maintain the zone
files. If you're
doing anything with DNS/BIND, you need this book.

h2n works like a champ - and no more 'hosts-xx' artifacts.



