In article <joseph.b.gurman-3009961844230001@eddy.nascom.nasa.gov>,
joseph.b.gurman@gsfc.nasa.gov (Joseph B. Gurman) wrote:

>    System: AlphaStation 250 4/266, firmware rev. level 3.6, Digital UNIX
3.2D-1
>
>    What I did: replace 8-bit card that came with system with PowerStorm
>4D20 (24-bit, Z buffer) card. The console displays OK, so I know I have
>the jumper and rotary switch settings on the card OK, BUT the window
>manager doesn' t launch.
>
>    What I can see: kern.log contains the following line:
>
>"Model 1011:d not in pci_option data table, can't configure"
>
>    Sorry to be so dense, but where is the pci_options table, and how do I
>go about getting an appropriate entry in it for the 4D20?
>
>    Aside for those not made physically ill by the mention of VMS: I
>installed one of these cards in an AlphaStation 600 5/266 running OVMS 6.2
>yesterday, and got a not dissimilar result. With CSC's help, I managed to
>find out that I needed to load Open3D (regardless of whether I had a
>license for it) so the driver would be where the OS was looking for it. Is
>there a parallel here?

    Ulp. I hate to respond to my own post, but in case anyone else should
find themselves in this boat, the solution _is_ analogous to that for
OVMS: load the software set for Open3D, specifically the baset set and the
set for the 4D20 (sets 1 and 14, if memory serves, in the Open3D 3.4
installation from the Layered Products CD's using cdmenu). You don't need
the Open3D license, just the software.

    Under Digital UNIX 4.0, you still need to load the base set from the
Open3D 4.0 set, but the driver ought to part of the OS --- or so says DEC
CSC.

    Thanks to those who responded by other means,

                  Joe Gurman

