In article <32528391.7E19@sensor.com>, Ron Natalie  <ron@sensor.com> wrote:
> > Has Digital fixed the VM system in 4.0 to allocate memory to processes from
> > the total virtual memory allowed, less some slack space, rather than either
> > not allocating at all or preallocating swap only? 
> 
> If you're talking about OSF1 (presently Digital UNIX), it's been
> configurable
> for a long time.  Read the "swapon" manpage.

*sigh*

That lets you configure whether memory is not allocated at all (deferred
mode) or preallocated from swap (immediate mode). It doesn't give you the
option of saying "when I allocate VM, allocate it from my total available
virtual memory (real memory + swap) rather then dedicating a chunk of swap
for me".

If you have a system with 256M real memory, say, and a 2GB disk, it would be
nice to be able to allocate 256M of swap, get a total of 512M of VM, but
not blow out random programs when you ran out of VM. To get 512M of usable
VM, you need to allocate 512M of disk. That's 1/4 of your disk space!
