Forgive me if this is a simple question.  I've been out of the Unix game for a
few years, and what used to work doesn't seem to anymore.

I am trying to vdump an advanced filesystem (advfs) on a remote node to a tape
drive on a local node using a command like:

	rsh foo.bar.edu '/sbin/vdump -0 -v -f - /usr' \
          | dd of=/dev/nrmt0h obs=65536

Trying to vrestore this doesn't work.

The following command DOES work:

	vdump -v -0 -f - /usr | dd of=/dev/nrmt0h obs=65536

When I vrestore this, I am able to read the file on tape.  Hence, I know the
obs=65536 is correct in dd (at least for this incarnation).

I have distilled the problem down to the following:

	This works:
		/sbin/vdump -0 -f - /usr | vrestore -t -f -

	This does NOT work:
		rsh foo.bar.edu '/sbin/vdump -0 -f - /usr' | vrestore -t -f -

It would seem that rsh is doing something to the output of vdump.  Does
anybody know what is going on here, and how I should fix it?

