>In article <3251D48D.696E@cyllene.uwa.edu.au>,
>Barney Dalton  <barneyd@cyllene.uwa.edu.au> wrote:
>>Hi,
>>I've just set up NIS on two alphas, one as a master and the other a
>>slave. The master appears to start fine and all the yp daemons are
>>running. when I start the slave ( I tried it as a client as well) I get
>>the following error message :
>>
>>ypbind : Secure mode sunos 3.x servers rejected
>>ypbind : Can't register TCP service
>>ypbind : Going down on signal 6.
>
>It looks like you're not running portmap, and ypbind needs it to register
>itself (try /usr/sbin/rpcinfo -p master).  portmap should be started
>by the script that is starting ypbind, so it looks like you need to
>investigate why portmap is not running.
>

BTW: "Secure mode sunos 3.x servers rejected" is not an error; it's telling
you that ypbind is running in "secure mode" (ypbind -s) which offers a
marginal increase in security (it won't talk to servers on non-root ports).
Of course, this does no good at all if there is any machine on your net
you don't control...

zw



